@@ -15,6 +15,16 @@ Use Herdr as an automation layer for coding agents. A script can control them, o
 
 A pane exists whether or not it contains an agent. An agent is the recognized process currently running inside a pane. `agent start` therefore requires an existing shell pane and never creates, splits, or moves layout.
 
+Automation input has a persistent target separate from the current process. Starting or recognizing an agent reserves its terminal for agent input. Raw `pane send-text`, `pane send-keys`, and `pane run` reject with `agent_input_protected`, including after the agent exits. Use `agent prompt` and `agent send-keys` for a live agent. Human TUI input is unaffected.
+
+To deliberately reuse an exited agent's lane for shell commands, read `pane get` and explicitly release the observed terminal:
+
+```bash
+herdr pane input-target <pane_id> terminal --terminal <terminal_id>
+```
+
+Release requires the same terminal identity, no live or launching agent, and an idle shell. Use `agent` instead of `terminal` to reserve an empty lane before launching. Protection follows its terminal through pane moves, shell respawn, session restore, and live handoff. A refusal never retries through a raw input API; keep the durable task or message pending. Old screen output is history, not a live-agent check.
+
 Creating a workspace also creates its first tab and root pane; creating a tab creates its root pane. Use the returned pane ID for the first process, and split only when that layout needs another terminal.
 
 Creation commands print JSON. Capture IDs from the response instead of predicting them:
